.Dd October 3, 2026
.Dt GETTIMEOFDAY 2
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm gettimeofday ,
.Nm settimeofday ,
.Nm clock_gettime ,
.Nm clock_settime ,
.Nm clock_getres ,
.Nm stime ,
.Nm times ,
.Nm utimensat
.Nd read and set clocks, and set file timestamps
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In sys/time.h
.Ft int
.Fn gettimeofday "struct timeval *restrict tv" "void *restrict tz"
.Ft int
.Fn settimeofday "const struct timeval *tv" "const struct timezone *tz"
.In time.h
.Ft int
.Fn clock_gettime "clockid_t clockid" "struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_settime "clockid_t clockid" "const struct timespec *tp"
.Ft int
.Fn stime "const time_t *t"
.Ft int
.Fn clock_getres "clockid_t clockid" "struct timespec *res"
.In sys/times.h
.Ft clock_t
.Fn times "struct tms *buf"
.In sys/stat.h
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn utimensat "int dirfd" "const char *path" "const struct timespec times[2]" "int flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn gettimeofday
reports real time in
.Fa tv ,
as seconds and microseconds.
The
.Fa tz
argument is ignored.
Real time is the CMOS clock reading taken at boot plus the time since,
counted in timer ticks with the PIT counter's place within the current tick
added, to the microsecond.
If the clock could not be read, the result is time since boot.
The hardware clock is read once; no service corrects the clock afterward.
.Pp
.Fn clock_settime
of
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Fn settimeofday
and
.Fn stime
set the real time, for a privileged caller alone.
The difference from the time the clock keeps becomes a step that every
later reading of real time adds, so the clock goes on from the new time at
the same rate, and a reading after a step back may be earlier than one
before it.
The boot time that
.Pa /proc/stat
gives moves with the step, as on Linux.
The hardware clock is not written, so the next boot reads the CMOS again.
.Fn settimeofday
keeps no time zone: one with only
.Fa tz ,
or with neither, sets nothing and succeeds.
Their i386 system call numbers are 264, with a 64-bit timespec 404, for
.Fn clock_settime ,
79 for
.Fn settimeofday
and 25 for
.Fn stime .
.Pp
.Fn clock_gettime
stores seconds and nanoseconds in
.Fa tp .
The clocks are:
.Bl -tag -width "CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID"
.It Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
ID 0.
The same real time reported by
.Fn gettimeofday .
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
ID 1.
Time since boot, read from uptime seconds, the ticks within the current
second and the PIT counter's place within the tick, as real time reads it.
A reading is never earlier than the one before it, and setting the real
time does not move it.
The clock continues across the 32-bit tick counter's wrap at about 497 days.
.It Dv CLOCK_PROCESS_CPUTIME_ID
ID 2.
The CPU time the timer has charged to the caller, user and system time
together, as
.Fn times
counts it.
.It Dv CLOCK_THREAD_CPUTIME_ID
ID 3.
The calling thread's own CPU time; ID 2 reads that of all the process's
threads, those that have ended included.
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW , CLOCK_BOOTTIME
IDs 4 and 7.
The same time since boot as
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC :
no service adjusts the clock's rate, and the machine is never suspended.
.It Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_COARSE
ID 6.
Time since boot at the last tick, without the PIT counter's place within it.
.It Dv CLOCK_REALTIME_COARSE
ID 5.
Real time at the last tick, without the PIT counter's place within it, so
never later than
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME
read at the same moment.
.El
.Pp
A negative clock ID names a CPU clock by process or thread ID, in the
encoding
.Fn clock_getcpuclockid
returns: the bitwise complement of the ID shifted left three places, bit 2
set for a thread, and in the low two bits the time read, 1 for user time
alone and 0 or 2 for user and system time together.
An ID of 0 in it names the caller.
A process clock may name any process that has not been reaped, by its
process ID; a thread clock may name any thread of the caller's process.
.Pp
.Fn clock_getres
stores a clock's resolution in
.Fa res
unless it is null: 1 microsecond for
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC ,
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW
and
.Dv CLOCK_BOOTTIME ,
and the 10 ms tick for every other clock, the coarse and CPU clocks.
Each clock advances in steps of its resolution.
.Pp
.Fn times
returns the monotonic tick count in units of 1/100 second, as a signed
32-bit value that goes negative after about 249 days.
If
.Fa buf
is nonnull, it receives the CPU time of the caller's process, all its
threads', in the same units:
.Va tms_utime
and
.Va tms_stime
are the ticks the timer charged to its threads in user mode and in the
kernel, and
.Va tms_cutime
and
.Va tms_cstime
are the same two totals for the children the process has reaped with
.Xr wait 2 ,
each with the children those children reaped.
The timer charges each tick to the thread it interrupts, as user time
when that thread was in its own code and as system time when it was in
the kernel, and a tick that finds the processor idle to no thread.
The times are therefore samples: a process that runs only between ticks
is charged nothing for that time, as on a Linux kernel built with tick-based
accounting.
They start at zero in a child of
.Xr fork 2
and are kept across
.Xr execve 2 .
A null
.Fa buf
is allowed.
.Ss File timestamps
.Fn utimensat
sets the access and modification times of
.Fa path .
An absolute path ignores
.Fa dirfd .
A relative path is resolved against the current working directory when
.Fa dirfd
is
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
or against the directory referred to by
.Fa dirfd
otherwise.
A path that ends in a slash, or whose last component is
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&.. ,
names a directory: a final symbolic link is followed even under
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ,
and anything but a directory returns
.Er ENOTDIR ,
as on Linux.
.Pp
A null
.Fa path
names the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to; GNU libc's and musl's
.Fn futimens
are this call.
It takes no flags and returns
.Er EINVAL
for any; with
.Dv AT_FDCWD
it returns
.Er EFAULT .
With
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
an empty
.Fa path
names the file
.Fa dirfd
refers to, or the current working directory for
.Dv AT_FDCWD .
A descriptor on a pipe, a socket, a terminal, a device or a synthetic
directory has no stored times and returns
.Er EROFS ;
Linux sets the times of such a file.
.Pp
The
.Fa times
argument points to two timespecs: access time first, modification time second.
A null pointer sets both to the current time.
For either element, a
.Va tv_nsec
of
.Dv UTIME_NOW
selects the current time;
.Dv UTIME_OMIT
leaves that timestamp unchanged.
When both elements specify
.Dv UTIME_OMIT ,
neither timestamp nor the change time is changed, and the call returns 0
before it looks at the path, the descriptor or the flags, as Linux does.
.Pp
Setting both timestamps to the current time requires ownership, write
permission on the file, or effective user ID 0.
Any other change requires ownership or effective user ID 0.
Omitting both timestamps requires neither.
.Pp
The
.Fa flags
argument may have
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW ,
which changes a symbolic link's own timestamps instead of following it,
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH .
Other bits return
.Er EINVAL .
A
.Va tv_nsec
other than
.Dv UTIME_NOW ,
.Dv UTIME_OMIT
or 0 to 999999999 returns
.Er EINVAL .
.Pp
Timestamp changes are supported on KRFFS, ext2, FAT32, and exFAT,
subject to the file system's stored precision.
On KRFFS, FAT32, and exFAT, a successful request on the volume's root
directory leaves its timestamps unchanged.
The root directory of the KRFFS boot file system accepts these requests
without checking ownership; its other nodes undergo the checks above.
Synthetic files under
.Pa /dev
and
.Pa /proc
return
.Er EROFS
unless both timestamps are omitted.
.Ss System call interface
The i386 system call numbers are 78 for
.Fn gettimeofday ,
265 for
.Fn clock_gettime ,
266 for
.Fn clock_getres ,
43 for
.Fn times ,
and 320 for
.Fn utimensat .
The time structures in this kernel interface have 32-bit seconds fields.
The signed 32-bit seconds field overflows in 2038 for real time and after
about 68 years since boot for monotonic time.
.Pp
musl and glibc first try
.Fn clock_gettime64
.Pq system call 403 ,
which reads the same clock into a timespec with 64-bit fields, as
.Fn clock_getres_time64
.Pq system call 406
reads the resolution; their seconds are those of the 32-bit calls.
musl calls system call 266 for
.Fn clock_getres
directly.
musl implements
.Fn gettimeofday
through
.Fn clock_gettime ;
it accepts a null
.Fa tv
and returns 0.
The raw
.Fn gettimeofday
system call requires a writable, nonnull timeval.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn gettimeofday ,
.Fn clock_gettime ,
.Fn clock_getres ,
and
.Fn utimensat
return 0 on success.
.Fn times
returns the tick count.
Kernel failures return a negative error number; the system call wrappers
return -1 and set
.Va errno ,
except musl's
.Fn times ,
which returns the kernel's result as it is: an invalid buffer gives the
raw -14 of
.Er EFAULT .
musl's
.Fn gettimeofday
returns 0 and writes its result in user space.
.Sh EXAMPLES
The default suite's
.Pa tests/timeprobe.c
reads
.Dv CLOCK_MONOTONIC
twice and checks that the second result is not earlier than the first.
.Pa tests/miscprobe.c
checks that no time
.Fn times
reports is negative or more than the ticks since boot, and
.Pa tests/musl/cputimemusl.c
checks that a loop in user mode gains user time, a loop of reads gains
system time, and a reaped child's time is added to the children's totals.
.Pa tests/musl/clockidmusl.c
reads every clock and its resolution, and a child's CPU clock through
.Fn clock_getcpuclockid .
.Pa tests/musl/fdcallmusl.c
sets a file's times through a null path and through an empty one with
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
and checks the refusals of a null path.
.Sh ERRORS
The kernel handlers return the following errors:
.Bl -tag -width "[ENAMETOOLONG]"
.It Bq Er EACCES
For
.Fn utimensat ,
an ancestor directory denies search permission, or a caller that is
neither the owner nor privileged tries to set both times to the current
time without write permission.
.It Bq Er EBADF
.Fn utimensat
was given an invalid directory descriptor for a relative path, or the
descriptor refers to a removed KRFFS boot-root directory; or, with a null
path or an empty one and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
the descriptor is invalid or refers to a KRFFS boot-root file that is gone;
or, with a null path, the descriptor was opened with
.Dv O_PATH .
.It Bq Er EBUSY
The
.Fn utimensat
path enters a mount being mounted or unmounted.
.It Bq Er EFAULT
A required buffer cannot be read or written.
The raw
.Fn gettimeofday
timeval or
.Fn clock_gettime
timespec is null, or a nonnull
.Fn clock_getres
timespec cannot be written.
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the path is null with
.Dv AT_FDCWD ,
or there is no current process.
.It Bq Er EINVAL
.Fn clock_settime
was given a clock other than
.Dv CLOCK_REALTIME ,
or a time-setting call a time before 1970 or a fraction outside a second;
or
.Fa clockid
is 8 or greater, including Linux's alarm clocks and
.Dv CLOCK_TAI ,
a clock of a descriptor, a CPU clock of no process that has not been
reaped, a process CPU clock named by an ID that is not a process ID, or a
thread CPU clock of another process; or
.Fn utimensat
has flag bits other than
.Dv AT_SYMLINK_NOFOLLOW
and
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
any flag with a null path, or a
.Va tv_nsec
out of range.
The clock is checked before the timespec is written.
.It Bq Er EIO
For
.Fn utimensat ,
the backing device has been removed, or a file-system read or metadata
write fails.
.It Bq Er ELOOP
The
.Fn utimensat
path requires more than 40 symbolic links to be followed.
.It Bq Er ENAMETOOLONG
The
.Fn utimensat
path, a component, a reconstructed directory path, or an expanded
symbolic-link path exceeds the resolver's buffer limit.
.It Bq Er ENOENT
The
.Fn utimensat
path is empty without
.Dv AT_EMPTY_PATH ,
or a component does not exist.
.It Bq Er ENOMEM
The file system cannot allocate a node while resolving the
.Fn utimensat
path, or the kernel has no page to copy the path into.
.It Bq Er ENOTDIR
A component of the
.Fn utimensat
path is not a directory, a descriptor used as the base of a relative
path is not a directory, or the path ends in a slash or in
.Pa \&.
or
.Pa \&..
and reaches something else.
.It Bq Er EPERM
A caller that is not privileged sets the real time; or a caller that is
neither the owner nor privileged requests a timestamp change other than
setting both times to the current time; or the file is immutable or
append-only, where Linux lets an append-only file have its times set to
the current time.
.It Bq Er EROFS
.Fn utimensat
targets a synthetic file, or a descriptor on a pipe, a socket, a
terminal, a device or a synthetic directory, or a timestamp update
reaches a read-only mount or a file system without a timestamp-setting
operation.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr nanosleep 2 ,
.Xr stat 2 ,
.Xr wait 2
